# Node.js TypeScript Best Practices

This document provides a comprehensive set of best practices for Node.js TypeScript projects based on [goldbergyoni/nodebestpractices](https://github.com/goldbergyoni/nodebestpractices).

## Project Structure

### Structure by Components

Organize your code around business components rather than technical roles.

```
my-system/
├─ apps/ (components)
│  ├─ users/
│  ├─ orders/
│  ├─ payments/
├─ libraries/ (generic cross-component functionality)
│  ├─ logger/
│  ├─ authenticator/
```

Each component should have its own folders for routes, models, services, etc. This promotes modularity and keeps related code together.

### Layer Your Components

Separate your code into the following layers:
- Entry-point layer (controllers/API routes)
- Domain layer (business logic)  
- Data-access layer (database interactions)

Keep web layer concerns separate from pure business logic.

### Wrap Common Utilities as Packages

Place reusable modules in dedicated folders with their own package.json. This improves maintainability and allows for future publishing.

### Use Hierarchical Configuration

For configuration management:
- Support reading from both files AND environment variables
- Keep secrets outside committed code
- Use typed configuration with validation
- Specify defaults for each key
- Use hierarchical config for easier organization

Consider using libraries like convict, env-var, or zod.

## Error Handling

### Use Async-Await or Promises

Avoid callback patterns in favor of async/await and promises.

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
function getUser(id: string, callback: (error: Error, user: User) => void) {
  // Implementation
}

// ✅ Better
async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Extend the Built-In Error Object

Create app-specific error classes that extend the built-in Error.

```typescript
export class AppError extends Error {
  public readonly name: string;
  public readonly httpCode: number;
  public readonly isOperational: boolean;

  constructor(name: string, httpCode: number, description: string, isOperational: boolean) {
    super(description);
    this.name = name;
    this.httpCode = httpCode;
    this.isOperational = isOperational;
    
    Error.captureStackTrace(this);
  }
}
```

### Distinguish Between Operational and Programmer Errors

- Operational errors: Known cases that can be handled (e.g., invalid input)
- Programmer errors (bugs): Unknown failures requiring application restart

### Handle Errors Centrally

Create a dedicated error-handling middleware to process all errors in one place.

```typescript
app.use((err: Error, req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  const errorHandler = new ErrorHandler();
  errorHandler.handleError(err, res);
});
```

### Document API Errors

For RESTful APIs, document errors using OpenAPI/Swagger. For GraphQL, use schema and comments.

### Exit Gracefully on Unhandled Errors

When unknown catastrophic errors occur, log the error, close connections, and restart the process.

### Use a Mature Logger

Choose a robust logging solution like Pino or Winston rather than console.log.

```typescript
import pino from 'pino';
const logger = pino({
  level: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? 'info' : 'debug',
});

logger.info('Application started');
```

### Test Error Flows

Test not just happy paths but also error scenarios in your test suite.

### Catch Unhandled Promise Rejections

Register handlers for unhandled rejections.

```typescript
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
  console.error('Unhandled Rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);
  // Application specific logging, throwing an error, or other logic here
});
```

### Fail Fast with Validation

Validate inputs early with libraries like ajv, zod, or typebox.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const userSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  name: z.string().min(2).max(100),
  age: z.number().min(18).optional(),
});

function createUser(data: unknown) {
  const validatedData = userSchema.parse(data);
  // Now proceed with validated data
}
```

## Code Style

### Use ESLint

Configure ESLint with Node.js specific plugins like eslint-plugin-node.

```json
// .eslintrc.json
{
  "extends": [
    "eslint:recommended",
    "plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended",
    "plugin:node/recommended"
  ],
  "plugins": ["@typescript-eslint", "node"],
  "parser": "@typescript-eslint/parser",
  "parserOptions": {
    "ecmaVersion": 2020,
    "sourceType": "module"
  }
}
```

### Use Consistent Naming Conventions

- Class names: PascalCase
- Functions and variables: camelCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- File names: kebab-case.ts

### Prefer const and let over var

Use `const` for values that won't be reassigned, `let` for those that will, and avoid `var`.

### Import Modules at the Top

Keep all imports at the top of files, outside any functions.

### Use === Over ==

Prefer strict equality to avoid unexpected type conversions.

### Use Arrow Functions for Callbacks

Maintain lexical context with arrow functions for cleaner code.

```typescript
// ❌ Traditional function
setTimeout(function() {
  this.doSomething();
}.bind(this), 1000);

// ✅ Arrow function
setTimeout(() => {
  this.doSomething();
}, 1000);
```

### Avoid Effects Outside of Functions

Don't put code with side effects (network calls, DB operations) outside of functions.

## Testing

### Write API/Component Tests

Prioritize testing at the API level for greatest coverage with minimal effort.

### Follow the AAA Pattern

Structure tests with Arrange, Act, Assert.

```typescript
describe('User service', () => {
  it('should create a new user when valid data is provided', async () => {
    // Arrange
    const userData = { name: 'Test User', email: 'test@example.com' };
    
    // Act
    const user = await userService.createUser(userData);
    
    // Assert
    expect(user).toHaveProperty('id');
    expect(user.name).toBe(userData.name);
  });
});
```

### Use Isolated Test Data

Avoid shared fixtures; each test should set up its own data.

### Unify Node Versions

Use tools like nvm, volta, or .nvmrc to ensure consistent Node.js versions.

### Test Middlewares in Isolation

Test Express middlewares without spinning up the entire server.

```typescript
import { authMiddleware } from './auth-middleware';

describe('Auth Middleware', () => {
  it('should call next() if token is valid', () => {
    const req = { headers: { authorization: 'Bearer valid-token' } };
    const res = {};
    const next = jest.fn();
    
    authMiddleware(req, res, next);
    
    expect(next).toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});
```

## Production Practices

### Implement Monitoring

Set up comprehensive monitoring covering:
- Uptime
- Application metrics
- Resource usage (CPU, memory)
- Error rates
- Request latency

### Use Smart Logging

Configure structured logging with correlation IDs to track requests.

### Use a Reverse Proxy

Delegate CPU-intensive operations (gzip, SSL) to nginx, HAProxy, or cloud services.

### Lock Dependencies

Use package-lock.json or yarn.lock to ensure consistent installations.

### Utilize All CPU Cores

In production, ensure your app can use all available CPU cores with tools like PM2.

### Measure and Guard Memory Usage

Monitor memory usage to detect leaks and set appropriate limits.

### Set NODE_ENV=production

This enables optimizations in many libraries, including Express.

```
NODE_ENV=production node app.js
```

### Log to stdout

Write logs to stdout and let the infrastructure handle log routing.

### Use npm ci for Installation

Prefer `npm ci` over `npm install` in CI environments for deterministic builds.

## Security

### Use Security Linters

Configure ESLint with security plugins like eslint-plugin-security.

### Implement Rate Limiting

Protect against brute force and DOS attacks with rate limiting.

```typescript
import rateLimit from 'express-rate-limit';

const apiLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100, // limit each IP to 100 requests per windowMs
  message: 'Too many requests from this IP, please try again later'
});

app.use('/api/', apiLimiter);
```

### Manage Secrets Securely

Store secrets in environment variables or a secure vault, never in code.

### Use ORM/ODM Libraries

Protect against query injection with proper ORM/ODM libraries.

```typescript
// ❌ Dangerous
db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`);

// ✅ Safe with ORM
const user = await User.findOne({ where: { email } });
```

### Set Secure HTTP Headers

Use libraries like helmet to set security headers.

```typescript
import helmet from 'helmet';
app.use(helmet());
```

### Validate Incoming JSON

Use schema validation for all API inputs.

### Use bcrypt or scrypt for Passwords

Never store plaintext passwords, use proper hashing.

```typescript
import * as bcrypt from 'bcrypt';

async function hashPassword(password: string): Promise<string> {
  const salt = await bcrypt.genSalt(10);
  return bcrypt.hash(password, salt);
}
```

### Escape HTML and JS Output

Prevent XSS by escaping output appropriately.

### Limit Payload Size

Protect against DOS attacks with payload size limits.

```typescript
app.use(express.json({ limit: '1mb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ limit: '1mb', extended: true }));
```

### Avoid eval() and new Function()

Never use eval() or Function constructor with user input.

### Run Node.js as Non-Root User

In production, run Node with limited privileges.

## Docker Practices

### Use Multi-Stage Builds

Create lean production images by using multi-stage builds.

```dockerfile
FROM node:16 AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

FROM node:16-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist
COPY --from=builder /app/node_modules ./node_modules
COPY --from=builder /app/package*.json ./
USER node
CMD ["node", "dist/main.js"]
```

### Bootstrap Using node Command

Start containers with the node command rather than npm scripts.

### Use .dockerignore

Prevent sensitive files from being copied to Docker images.

```
node_modules
npm-debug.log
.env
.git
.gitignore
```

### Clean Dependencies Before Production

Remove dev dependencies in production builds.

### Set Memory Limits

Configure memory limits in both Docker and Node.js.

### Use Explicit Image Tags

Never use the "latest" tag; specify exact versions.

### Scan Images for Vulnerabilities

Use tools to scan Docker images for security issues.

## Performance

### Don't Block the Event Loop

Move CPU-intensive tasks to worker threads or separate services.

### Prefer Native JS Methods

Use built-in JavaScript methods instead of utility libraries when possible.

```typescript
// ❌ Using lodash
import _ from 'lodash';
const unique = _.uniq(array);

// ✅ Using native methods
const unique = [...new Set(array)];
```

## TypeScript Specific

### Use TypeScript Sparingly

Aim for simple types and use advanced features only when needed.

```typescript
// ❌ Overly complex
type DeepReadonly<T> = {
  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P] extends object ? DeepReadonly<T[P]> : T[P];
};

// ✅ Simple and clear
interface User {
  readonly id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}
```

### Define Return Types

Explicitly define return types of functions for better documentation and type checking.

```typescript
// ❌ Implicit return type
function getUser(id: string) {
  // ...
}

// ✅ Explicit return type
function getUser(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
  // ...
}
```

### Use Interface for Public APIs

Prefer interfaces for public APIs and types for complex types, unions, or intersections.

### Enable Strict Type Checking

Configure TypeScript with strict mode enabled.

```json
// tsconfig.json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strictFunctionTypes": true,
    "strictBindCallApply": true
  }
}
```

### Import Built-in Modules with node: Protocol

Use the node: protocol for built-in modules.

```typescript
// ❌ Without protocol
import { createServer } from 'http';

// ✅ With protocol
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
```
